## 4. Low‑bandwidth and performance strategy

This is your key differentiator; treat it as a first‑class requirement, not an afterthought.  

Core principles (all drawn from low‑bandwidth design research):  

- **HTML‑first, minimal JS:** Use server‑side rendering so the first paint is almost pure HTML; heavy JS and interactive extras load later or not at all. [medium](https://medium.com/@ititans_tech/designing-for-low-bandwidth-users-optimizing-websites-for-all-e2a78887e586)
- **Page weight budget:** Aim for **< 25–50 KB HTML per page** for core content (excluding occasional images), aligning with classic “no page bigger than 25 KB” low‑bandwidth guidelines. [columbia](http://www.columbia.edu/itc/sipa/nelson/newmediadev08/Key%20Recommendations%20for%20Designing%20Web%20Pages%20for%20Low%20Bandwidth.html)
- **Image strategy:**  
  - Default to **no thumbnails** on the lowest‑bandwidth mode; show only text, source, and time. [aptivate](https://aptivate.org/en/services/resources/web-design-guidelines/index.html)
  - On normal mode, use small, aggressively compressed WebP thumbnails and **lazy‑load** them as the user scrolls. [birdhousewebsites](https://birdhousewebsites.com/blog/designing-for-low-bandwidth-users-essential-tips-and/)
- **CSS/JS optimization:**  
  - Single, minified CSS bundle; avoid big UI libraries.  
  - JS only for things that really need it (infinite scroll, offline caching, PWA shell); minify and load it async/defer. [linkedin](https://www.linkedin.com/advice/1/how-can-you-design-low-bandwidth-optimized-website-zwvxc)
- **Caching:**  
  - Strong HTTP caching + CDN for static assets.  
  - On client, use service worker to cache last N feeds so app is usable even with flaky connectivity. [aptivate](https://aptivate.org/en/services/resources/web-design-guidelines/index.html)
- **Optional text‑only mode:** Provide a toggle (or automatic detection) for a “Text‑only” view: no images, no animations, simplified typography; historically recommended for low‑bandwidth contexts. [columbia](http://www.columbia.edu/itc/sipa/nelson/newmediadev08/Key%20Recommendations%20for%20Designing%20Web%20Pages%20for%20Low%20Bandwidth.html)

With your skills, you can easily keep Core Web Vitals excellent if you design to these constraints from day 1.

